\ Cross compiler to produce a CV8 binary image of RelF (Relative
\ Forth) from kernel.4. Runs on a CV8 image: see README.md for the
\ bootstrap, and CV8-REFERENCE.md for the format it emits.
\ Based on SOD32 by L.C. Benschop
\ Copyright 2001 - 2005, Kirill Timofeev, kt97679@gmail.com
\ The program is released under the GNU General Public License version 2.
\ There is NO WARRANTY.

\ Target cell width in bytes: 8 by default (x86-64, ARM64, ...). To
\ cross-compile for a 32-bit target (e.g. i386) instead, edit the "8"
\ below to "4" (see README.md) - this is deliberately a plain, direct
\ edit rather than something conditional/settable-before-including: an
\ earlier attempt at that used IF/THEN at the top level (interpret
\ mode) to skip re-creating the variable if the person had already set
\ one, but IF/THEN here are compile-only words - they only work inside
\ a :...; definition, where the compiler processes tokens up front. At
\ the top level they silently corrupt the dictionary instead of
\ erroring (found via PROGRESS.md's account of a long debugging session
\ - see it before reintroducing anything like this). Everything
\ cell-width-dependent in this file and in kernel.4 derives from this
\ one place.
VARIABLE TARGET-CELL-BYTES
8 TARGET-CELL-BYTES !

\ PART 1: THE VOCABULARIES.

VOCABULARY TARGET
\ This vocabulary will hold shadow definitions for all words that are in
\ the target dictionary. When a shadow definition is executed, it
\ performs the compile action in the target dictionary.

VOCABULARY TRANSIENT
\ This vocabulary will hold definitions that must be executed by the
\ host system ( the system on which the cross compiler runs) and that
\ compile to the target system.

\ Expl: The word IF occurs in all three vocabularies. The word IF in the
\       FORTH vocabulary is run by the host system and is used when
\       compiling host definitions. A different version is in the
\       TRANSIENT vocabulary. This one runs on the host system and
\       is used when compiling target definitions. The version in the
\       TARGET vocabulary is the version that will run on the target
\       system.


\ PART 2: THE TARGET DICTIONARY SPACE.

\ Next we need to define the target space and the words to access it.

40000 CONSTANT IMAGE_SIZE

CREATE IMAGE IMAGE_SIZE CHARS ALLOT \ This space contains the target image.
       IMAGE IMAGE_SIZE 0 FILL      \ Initialize it to zero.

\ Fetch and store characters in the target space.
: C@-T ( t-addr --- c) CHARS IMAGE + C@ ;
: C!-T ( c t-addr ---) CHARS IMAGE + C! ;

\ Fetch and store cells in the target space, TARGET-CELL-BYTES wide.
\ As of phase 5 (see GOALS.md), images are native host endianness, not a
\ portable on-disk format. These build/take apart a little-endian value
\ byte by byte via C@-T/C!-T (not plain @/! on this host), because
\ that's what makes the *disk file* end up in the intended byte order:
\ WRITE-FILE (on whatever engine happens to be running this) reverses
\ whatever it writes, and C@-T/C!-T's own byte-level access already
\ cancels that reversal out (see PROGRESS.md for the derivation) - the
\ same reason name strings/PLACE-T never needed any special handling
\ either. Safe on this host regardless of TARGET-CELL-BYTES: this host's
\ own cells are always at least as wide as the target's (see PROGRESS.md,
\ phase 2, for what goes wrong when that's not true, and why building a
\ *narrower* target from a wider host - unlike phase 2's situation - has
\ no equivalent problem).
\ BEGIN/WHILE/REPEAT with an explicit counter, not DO/LOOP: DO/LOOP here
\ reliably hung the bootstrap somewhere past kernel.4 loading (found by
\ bisection, not fully root-caused - see PROGRESS.md). These are called
\ often enough, from enough different contexts, that avoiding DO/LOOP's
\ own return-stack usage entirely was the safer fix.
: @-T  ( t-addr --- x)
  0 0
  BEGIN
    DUP TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ <
  WHILE
    >R OVER R@ CHARS + C@-T R@ 8 * LSHIFT OR R> 1+
  REPEAT
  DROP SWAP DROP
  \ Sign-extend when the target cell is narrower than this host's own
  \ (this host's cells are always at least as wide as the target's, see
  \ the note on C@-T/C!-T above): the loop above only fills the low
  \ TARGET-CELL-BYTES bytes, leaving the high bytes zero regardless of
  \ the target value's actual sign. Negative target-level sentinels
  \ (FORWARD's own -1, used by RESOLVE's chain-termination check) need
  \ those high bytes properly extended to compare correctly against
  \ host -1 - otherwise RESOLVE's BEGIN/WHILE loop never sees them as
  \ equal and walks off the end of the chain into unrelated memory.
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 < IF
    DUP 1 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 * 1 - LSHIFT AND IF
      -1 TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 * LSHIFT OR
    THEN
  THEN ;
: !-T  ( x t-addr ---)
  0
  BEGIN
    DUP TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ <
  WHILE
    >R OVER R@ 8 * RSHIFT OVER R@ CHARS + C!-T R> 1+
  REPEAT
  DROP 2DROP ;

\ A dictionary is constructed in the target space. Here are the primitives
\ to maintain the dictionary pointer and to reserve space.

VARIABLE DP-T                       \ Dictionary pointer for target dictionary.
0 DP-T !                            \ Initialize it to zero, SOD starts at 0.
: THERE ( --- t-addr) DP-T @ ;      \ Equivalent of HERE in target space.
: ALLOT-T ( n --- ) DP-T +! ;       \ Reserve n bytes in the dictionary.
: CHARS-T ( n1 --- n2 ) ;
: CELLS-T ( n1 --- n2 ) TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ * ;  \ n1 target cells in bytes.
: ALIGN-T                           \ Target only accesses cells at aligned
                                    \ addresses.
  BEGIN THERE TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 1- AND WHILE 1 ALLOT-T REPEAT ;
: ALIGNED-T ( n1 --- n2 )
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 1- + TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ NEGATE AND ;
: C,-T  ( c --- )  THERE C!-T 1 CHARS ALLOT-T ;
: ,-T   ( x --- )  THERE !-T  1 CELLS-T ALLOT-T ;

: PLACE-T ( c-addr len t-addr --- ) \ Move counted string to target space.
  OVER OVER C!-T 1+ CHARS IMAGE + SWAP CHARS CMOVE ;

\ PART 3: CREATING NEW DEFINITIONS IN THE TARGET SYSTEM.

\ These words create new target definitions, both the shadow definition
\ and the header in the target dictionary. The layout of target headers
\ can be changed but FIND in the target system must be changed accordingly.

VARIABLE TLINK 0 TLINK !   \ This variable points to the name
                           \ of the last definition.

\ The target's word list is HASHED - see kernel.4's FORTH-WORDLIST - so
\ the cross-compiler must thread the dictionary it builds exactly the
\ way the target will search it. These are the host-side mirrors of the
\ target's thread heads and of its HASH.

32 CONSTANT #TTHREADS
CREATE TTHREADS #TTHREADS CELLS ALLOT
TTHREADS #TTHREADS CELLS 0 FILL

: THASH ( c-addr u --- n )
\ Must produce exactly what kernel.4's HASH produces for the same name
\ and the same thread count. If the two ever disagree a word is filed
\ under one thread and looked for under another, and the target cannot
\ find half its own dictionary.
  #TTHREADS >R
  OVER C@ 1 LSHIFT OVER 1 > IF ROT 1+ C@ 2 LSHIFT XOR ELSE ROT DROP
   THEN XOR
  R> 1- AND ;

VARIABLE THIS-THREAD
VARIABLE LINKCELL
VARIABLE NAMELEN

: PUBLISH-WORDLIST ( t-addr --- )
\ Copy the host-side thread heads into the target's word list array.
\ This is a definition rather than a run of interpreted code because
\ DO ... LOOP is compile-only; interpreting it segfaulted the host at
\ the very last step of the build, after everything else had worked.
  DUP #TTHREADS SWAP !-T             \ cell 0: the thread count
  #TTHREADS 0 DO
    I CELLS TTHREADS + @             ( wl head )
    OVER I 1+ CELLS-T + !-T          ( wl )
  LOOP
  DROP ;

: TLINK-LEN ( d --- n )
\ The bytes a link of distance d needs - kernel.4's LINK-LEN.
  DUP 128 U< IF DROP 1 EXIT THEN
  16384 U< IF 2 ELSE 3 THEN ;

: TLINK, ( d n --- )
\ Append a link of exactly n bytes, tag byte last - kernel.4's LINK,.
  DUP 1 = IF DROP C,-T EXIT THEN
  2 = IF DUP 255 AND C,-T 8 RSHIFT 128 OR C,-T EXIT THEN
  DUP 255 AND C,-T DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C,-T 16 RSHIFT 192 OR C,-T ;

VARIABLE LINKLEN

: "HEADER >IN @ CREATE >IN ! \ Create the shadow definition.
\ The target header is [link][count][name], nothing padded - see
\ kernel.4's HEADER, which this must lay out identically. LINKCELL
\ holds the previous name field in the thread, 0 if there is none: no
\ name field is ever at target address 0, where the prologue is.
  BL WORD COUNT               ( c-addr u )
  2DUP THASH THIS-THREAD !
  DUP NAMELEN !
  THIS-THREAD @ CELLS TTHREADS + @ LINKCELL !
  LINKCELL @ IF THERE 3 + LINKCELL @ - TLINK-LEN ELSE 1 THEN LINKLEN !
  LINKCELL @ IF THERE LINKLEN @ + LINKCELL @ - ELSE 0 THEN LINKLEN @ TLINK,
  THERE PLACE-T               \ Place name in target dictionary.
  THERE TLINK !
  THERE THIS-THREAD @ CELLS TTHREADS + !
  THERE C@-T 128 OR THERE C!-T \ Set bit 7 of count byte as a marker.
  NAMELEN @ 1+ ALLOT-T ;

ALSO TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS

: IMMEDIATE TLINK @ DUP C@-T 64 OR SWAP C!-T ;
            \ Set the IMMEDIATE bit of last name.

PREVIOUS DEFINITIONS


\ PART 4: CODE GENERATION - CV8 (CV8-REFERENCE.md)

\ The target is a byte stream: one-byte opcodes, two- or three-byte
\ calls, and the specialised opcodes the engine implements. Until
\ Iteration 243 this file emitted CELLS, and every CV8 image was
\ translated from that cell image by tools/layout.py after running it
\ on relf.c. Emitting CV8 here is what retired both.
\
\ Everything below is the host-side twin of the compiler in kernel.4:
\ the same encoding, the same peepholes, written against the target
\ space instead of HERE. When one changes, the other must.

VARIABLE STATE-T 0 STATE-T ! \ State variable for cross compiler.
: T] 1 STATE-T ! ;
: T[ 0 STATE-T ! ;

VARIABLE CSP   \ Stack pointer checking between : and ;
: !CSP DEPTH CSP ! ;
: ?CSP DEPTH CSP @ - ABORT" Incomplete control structure" ;

\ ---- the opcode map ------------------------------------------------
\ Primitive opcodes are kernel.4's PRIMITIVE order. The ones declared
\ after ESCAPED sit behind ESC + a selector, so only the first #DIRECT
\ take a byte of their own, and the synthetic opcodes start right after
\ them - an escaped primitive uses no opcode, and adding one moves
\ nothing (Iteration 247). Until then they started at the TOTAL count,
\ which wasted an opcode per escaped primitive. kernel.4 declares every
\ primitive before its first use of one of these.
VARIABLE #PRIMS  0 #PRIMS !
VARIABLE #DIRECT -1 #DIRECT !
VARIABLE PRIMS-USED 0 PRIMS-USED !
: DIRECT? ( n --- f ) #DIRECT @ 0< IF DROP -1 ELSE #DIRECT @ < THEN ;
: NSYN ( --- n )
\ The first synthetic opcode: the direct primitive count. From now on
\ that must not change - PRIMITIVE refuses once this has been used, or a
\ primitive declared after the first VARIABLE would silently renumber
\ every DOVAR before it.
  -1 PRIMS-USED ! #DIRECT @ DUP 0< IF DROP #PRIMS @ THEN ;
: LIT32-OP  ( --- n ) NSYN ;
: DOVAR-OP  ( --- n ) NSYN 1+ ;
: DODOES-OP ( --- n ) NSYN 2 + ;
: LIT8-OP   ( --- n ) NSYN 3 + ;
: LIT8X-OP  ( --- n ) NSYN 4 + ;
: FOLD-BASE ( --- n ) NSYN 5 + ;
\ The one-byte-offset branches follow the folded band (Iteration 258).
: BRANCH8-OP  ( --- n ) NSYN 5 + 23 + ;
: 0BRANCH8-OP ( --- n ) NSYN 5 + 24 + ;
1 CONSTANT EXIT-OP
2 CONSTANT LIT16-OP
3 CONSTANT BRANCH-OP
4 CONSTANT 0BRANCH-OP
\ Fixed, at the top of the byte.
 97 CONSTANT LIT0-OP     \ 98 push 1, 99 push -1
100 CONSTANT VAR@-OP
101 CONSTANT VAR!-OP
121 CONSTANT ADDI-OP     \ +1 is ADDI;EXIT
123 CONSTANT EQI-OP      \ +1 is EQI;EXIT
125 CONSTANT LIT64-OP
126 CONSTANT ESC-OP
: TCELLSHIFT ( --- n ) TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 = IF 3 ELSE 2 THEN ;
\ Calls and slot operands name a byte offset: the call scale is 0,
\ because bodies are not aligned (CV8-REFERENCE.md 3.4).

\ The primitives with a folded "then EXIT" form, in the order the
\ engine's table has them. Names, because their opcodes are not known
\ until kernel.4 declares them; PRIMITIVE fills in FOLD-IDX.
23 CONSTANT #FOLD
CREATE FOLD-IDX #FOLD CELLS ALLOT
: NAMES, ( n "name"... --- )
\ Lay down n counted strings. WORD parses to HERE, so ALLOT keeps it.
  0 DO BL WORD C@ 1+ ALLOT LOOP ;
: FOLD-SETUP ( --- )
  #FOLD 0 DO -1 FOLD-IDX I CELLS + ! LOOP ;
FOLD-SETUP
CREATE FOLD-NAMES
16 NAMES, + = ! @ LSHIFT RSHIFT C@ C! AND OR XOR LIT < U< OVER DROP
 7 NAMES, DUP SWAP ROT >R R> R@ NEGATE
ALIGN

: FOLD-POS ( c-addr u --- k | -1 )
\ Position of a name in the fold list, or -1.
  FOLD-NAMES #FOLD 0 DO           ( c-addr u p )
    >R 2DUP R@ COUNT COMPARE 0= IF 2DROP R> DROP I UNLOOP EXIT THEN
    R> COUNT +
  LOOP DROP 2DROP -1 ;

\ The four primitives the peepholes complete, by index, found the same
\ way when PRIMITIVE declares them.
VARIABLE PLUS-IDX   VARIABLE EQUALS-IDX
VARIABLE FETCH-IDX  VARIABLE STORE-IDX

\ ---- raw emission ---------------------------------------------------
: W,-T ( n --- )  DUP 255 AND C,-T 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C,-T ;
: 32,-T ( n --- ) DUP W,-T 16 RSHIFT W,-T ;
: REWIND-T ( t-addr --- ) THERE - ALLOT-T ;

\ ---- peephole state: see kernel.4's NO-PEEP --------------------------
VARIABLE LAST-OP-T
VARIABLE LAST-LIT-T   VARIABLE LAST-LITV-T
VARIABLE LAST-CALL-T  VARIABLE LAST-CALLXT-T
: NO-PEEP-T ( --- ) 0 LAST-OP-T ! 0 LAST-LIT-T ! 0 LAST-CALL-T ! ;
NO-PEEP-T
\ An image offset of 0 is the prologue, which no pattern ever starts at,
\ so 0 is a safe "none".

: SLOT,-T ( t-addr --- )
\ A slot operand: a byte offset like a call, 2 bytes big-endian, or 3
\ with the top bit set.
  DUP 32768 < IF DUP 8 RSHIFT C,-T 255 AND C,-T
  ELSE DUP 16 RSHIFT 128 OR C,-T DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C,-T 255 AND C,-T
  THEN ;

: CALLBYTES-T ( t-addr v --- )
\ Store the three-byte (far) call form for offset v at t-addr.
  2DUP 16 RSHIFT 192 OR SWAP C!-T
  2DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND SWAP 1+ C!-T
  255 AND SWAP 2 + C!-T ;

: CALL,-T ( t-addr --- )
\ A call to the target body at t-addr: near if it fits 14 bits.
  NO-PEEP-T THERE LAST-CALL-T ! DUP LAST-CALLXT-T !
  DUP 16384 < IF DUP 8 RSHIFT 128 OR C,-T 255 AND C,-T
  ELSE THERE SWAP CALLBYTES-T 3 ALLOT-T THEN ;

: SIGNED-T ( n --- n' )
\ A literal as the target sees it: on a 4-byte target, the low 32 bits
\ sign-extended, so that -1 and 0xFFFFFFFF are the same value.
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 4 = 1 CELLS 8 = AND IF
    4294967295 AND DUP 2147483648 AND IF 4294967296 - THEN
  THEN ;

: LITERAL-T ( n --- )
\ A literal in the narrowest form. Remembered for ADDI/EQI and for
\ folding into the EXIT.
  SIGNED-T
  NO-PEEP-T THERE LAST-LIT-T ! DUP LAST-LITV-T !
  DUP 0 = IF DROP LIT0-OP C,-T EXIT THEN
  DUP 1 = IF DROP LIT0-OP 1+ C,-T EXIT THEN
  DUP -1 = IF DROP LIT0-OP 2 + C,-T EXIT THEN
  DUP 256 U< IF THERE LAST-OP-T ! LIT8-OP C,-T C,-T EXIT THEN
  DUP 65536 U< IF THERE LAST-OP-T ! LIT16-OP C,-T W,-T EXIT THEN
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 4 = IF LIT32-OP C,-T 32,-T EXIT THEN
  DUP 2147483648 + 4294967296 U< IF LIT32-OP C,-T 32,-T EXIT THEN
  LIT64-OP C,-T DUP 32,-T 32 RSHIFT 32,-T ;

: FOLD-OP-T ( op --- op2 -1 | op 0 )
  DUP LIT8-OP = IF DROP LIT8X-OP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP ADDI-OP = OVER EQI-OP = OR IF 1+ -1 EXIT THEN
  #FOLD 0 DO
    DUP FOLD-IDX I CELLS + @ = IF DROP I FOLD-BASE + -1 UNLOOP EXIT THEN
  LOOP 0 ;

: EXIT,-T ( --- )
\ The end of a body: EXIT, folded into the last operation if it can be.
  LAST-LIT-T @ IF LAST-LITV-T @ 2 U< IF
    LAST-LIT-T @ REWIND-T THERE LAST-OP-T ! LIT8-OP C,-T LAST-LITV-T @ C,-T
  THEN THEN
  LAST-OP-T @ ?DUP IF
    DUP C@-T FOLD-OP-T IF SWAP C!-T ELSE 2DROP EXIT-OP C,-T THEN
  ELSE EXIT-OP C,-T THEN
  NO-PEEP-T ;

: PEEP-IMM-T ( idx --- f )
  LAST-LIT-T @ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LAST-LITV-T @ 128 + 256 U< 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP PLUS-IDX @ = IF DROP ADDI-OP ELSE
  EQUALS-IDX @ = IF EQI-OP ELSE 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-LIT-T @ REWIND-T LAST-LITV-T @ >R NO-PEEP-T
  THERE LAST-OP-T ! C,-T R> 255 AND C,-T -1 ;

: PEEP-VAR-T ( idx --- f )
  LAST-CALL-T @ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LAST-CALLXT-T @ C@-T DOVAR-OP = 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP FETCH-IDX @ = IF DROP VAR@-OP ELSE
  STORE-IDX @ = IF VAR!-OP ELSE 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-CALL-T @ REWIND-T LAST-CALLXT-T @ >R NO-PEEP-T
  C,-T R> 4 + ALIGNED-T SLOT,-T -1 ;   \ >BODY-T, which is defined later

: PRIM,-T ( idx --- )
\ Compile primitive number idx into the current target definition.
  DUP DIRECT? 0= IF NO-PEEP-T ESC-OP C,-T #DIRECT @ - C,-T EXIT THEN
  DUP PEEP-IMM-T IF DROP EXIT THEN
  DUP PEEP-VAR-T IF DROP EXIT THEN
  NO-PEEP-T THERE LAST-OP-T ! C,-T ;

\ ---- branches -------------------------------------------------------
\ A branch operand is a signed 16-bit offset from the operand itself.
: >MARK-T ( --- t-addr ) NO-PEEP-T THERE 0 W,-T ;
: >RESOLVE-T ( t-addr --- )
  NO-PEEP-T THERE OVER - SWAP 2DUP C!-T 1+ SWAP 8 RSHIFT SWAP C!-T ;
: <RESOLVE-T ( t-addr --- ) NO-PEEP-T THERE - W,-T ;
: BACK-T ( dest op16 op8 --- )
\ A backward branch: the one-byte-offset form when the distance fits.
\ kernel.4's BACK, is the same.
  NO-PEEP-T ROT THERE 1+ -              ( op16 op8 off )
  DUP -128 < IF
    SWAP DROP SWAP C,-T W,-T
  ELSE
    SWAP C,-T C,-T DROP
  THEN NO-PEEP-T ;
: OPERAND3-T ( n --- )   \ a three-byte operand, high byte first
  DUP 16 RSHIFT 255 AND C,-T DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C,-T 255 AND C,-T ;

CREATE IMAGE-HDR 8 ALLOT
VARIABLE IMAGE-FID
: HDR, ( x --- )
\ Write one TARGET cell. The host cell is at least as wide and the
\ host is little-endian, so its low bytes are the target cell.
  IMAGE-HDR ! IMAGE-HDR TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ IMAGE-FID @ WRITE-FILE
  ABORT" Can't write file" ;

: SAVE-IMAGE ( "name" --- )
\ The CV8 header (CV8-REFERENCE.md 5.1), then the image: the thread
\ heads, which are also in the image where COLD relocates them, and
\ the DOES> tail count, always 0 because DODOES carries its target
\ inline.
  32 WORD COUNT W/O BIN CREATE-FILE ABORT" Can't create file" IMAGE-FID !
  67 IMAGE-HDR C!  86 IMAGE-HDR 1+ C!  56 IMAGE-HDR 2 + C!   \ "CV8"
  48 IMAGE-HDR 3 + C!                                     \ call scale 0
  TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ IMAGE-HDR 4 + C!
  76 IMAGE-HDR 5 + C!         \ 'L': specialised opcodes
  4 IMAGE-HDR 6 + C!          \ format version
  15 IMAGE-HDR 7 + C!         \ varcall | varslot | spec | lit64
  IMAGE-HDR 8 IMAGE-FID @ WRITE-FILE ABORT" Can't write file"
  #TTHREADS HDR,
  #TTHREADS 0 DO I CELLS TTHREADS + @ HDR, LOOP
  0 HDR,
  IMAGE THERE IMAGE-FID @ WRITE-FILE ABORT" Can't write file"
  IMAGE-FID @ CLOSE-FILE ABORT" Can't close file" ;

: TNAME= ( c-addr u --- f )
\ Is the newest target name c-addr u?
  TLINK @ C@-T 31 AND OVER = IF
    TLINK @ 1+ IMAGE + OVER COMPARE 0=
  ELSE 2DROP 0 THEN ;

ONLY FORTH ALSO TRANSIENT DEFINITIONS FORTH
\ Now define the words that do compile code.

: LOCALS-CELLS ( --- )
\ Reserve the five locals cells the engine reads at image offset 8.
\ The two boot calls before them are three bytes each, so they end at
\ 6 and the cells start at 8 on either cell width.
  ALIGN-T THERE 8 - ABORT" cross: LOCALS-CELLS is not at offset 8"
  5 CELLS-T ALLOT-T ;

: ESCAPED ( --- )
\ Every PRIMITIVE after this one is reached through ESC.
  #PRIMS @ #DIRECT ! ;

: PRIMITIVE ( --- )
\ A primitive's body is its opcode (or ESC and a selector) then EXIT,
\ so EXECUTE works on it; compiling it inlines the opcode. The MACRO
\ bit (32) is what tells COMPILE, to inline.
  PRIMS-USED @ ABORT" cross: PRIMITIVE after the opcode map was used"
  #PRIMS @ 1 #PRIMS +!
  "HEADER THERE , DUP ,
  DUP DIRECT? IF DUP C,-T ELSE ESC-OP C,-T DUP #DIRECT @ - C,-T THEN
  EXIT-OP C,-T
  TLINK @ DUP C@-T 32 OR SWAP C!-T
  TLINK @ 1+ IMAGE + TLINK @ C@-T 31 AND FOLD-POS DUP 0< 0= IF
    OVER SWAP CELLS FOLD-IDX + !
  ELSE DROP THEN
  S" +" TNAME= IF DUP PLUS-IDX ! THEN
  S" =" TNAME= IF DUP EQUALS-IDX ! THEN
  S" @" TNAME= IF DUP FETCH-IDX ! THEN
  S" !" TNAME= IF DUP STORE-IDX ! THEN
  DROP
  DOES> CELL+ @ PRIM,-T ;

: OPCODE ( op "name" --- )
\ A kernel word the engine implements as an opcode of its own - the
\ fifteen tiny words of CV8-REFERENCE.md 7.4. Declared exactly like a
\ primitive, so COMPILE, inlines it; the engine, not a Forth body,
\ defines what it does.
  "HEADER THERE , DUP ,
  C,-T EXIT-OP C,-T
  TLINK @ DUP C@-T 32 OR SWAP C!-T
  DOES> CELL+ @ NO-PEEP-T THERE LAST-OP-T ! C,-T ;

\ Literals kernel.4's compiler needs, compiled into its definitions.
\ Opcode numbers above the primitives depend on how many there are, so
\ they cannot be written into kernel.4 as numbers.
: CELLBYTES-TOK TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ LITERAL-T ;
: CELLSHIFT-TOK TCELLSHIFT LITERAL-T ;
: SIGNSHIFT-TOK TARGET-CELL-BYTES @ 8 * 1- LITERAL-T ;
: LIT32-TOK  LIT32-OP LITERAL-T ;
: DOVAR-TOK  DOVAR-OP LITERAL-T ;
: DODOES-TOK DODOES-OP LITERAL-T ;
: LIT8-TOK   LIT8-OP LITERAL-T ;
: BRANCH8-TOK BRANCH8-OP LITERAL-T ;
: 0BRANCH8-TOK 0BRANCH8-OP LITERAL-T ;
: LIT8X-TOK  LIT8X-OP LITERAL-T ;
: FOLD-BASE-TOK FOLD-BASE LITERAL-T ;
: #FOLD-TOK  #FOLD LITERAL-T ;
: [OP] ( "name" --- )
\ Compile the opcode of a direct primitive as a literal.
  ALSO TARGET ' >BODY CELL+ @ PREVIOUS LITERAL-T ;
: FOLD-TABLE ( --- )
\ Overwrite the cell a VARIABLE just reserved with the fold list as
\ opcode bytes, for kernel.4's FOLD-OP.
  -1 CELLS-T ALLOT-T
  #FOLD 0 DO FOLD-IDX I CELLS + @ C,-T LOOP ALIGN-T ;
FORTH DEFINITIONS

\ PART 5: FORWARD REFERENCES

\ A forward reference is a three-byte far call whose target is not
\ known yet. Until RESOLVE, those three bytes hold the address of the
\ previous reference to the same word (22 bits, all ones at the end of
\ the chain) - the same threading the cell version did through cells.
\
\ FORWARD and RESOLVE are defined before : and ; are redefined below;
\ see PROGRESS.md for why a CREATE ... DOES> word cannot be compiled
\ under the redefined pair.

4194303 CONSTANT END-CHAIN

: FORWARD
  CREATE END-CHAIN ,
  DOES> NO-PEEP-T
        DUP @ THERE SWAP CALLBYTES-T THERE SWAP ! 3 ALLOT-T ;

: CHAIN@-T ( t-addr --- v )
  DUP C@-T 63 AND 16 LSHIFT OVER 1+ C@-T 8 LSHIFT OR SWAP 2 + C@-T OR ;

: RESOLVE
  ALSO TARGET >IN @ ' >BODY @ >R >IN !   \ the target body
  TRANSIENT ' >BODY @                    \ the head of the chain
  BEGIN DUP END-CHAIN - WHILE
    DUP CHAIN@-T SWAP R@ CALLBYTES-T
  REPEAT DROP R> DROP PREVIOUS ;

: T' ( --- t-addr) \ Find the execution token of a target definition.
  ALSO TARGET ' >BODY @ PREVIOUS ;

: >BODY-T ( t-addr1 --- t-addr2 ) \ the parameter field of a data word
  4 + ALIGNED-T ;       \ kernel.4's >BODY

